    "chunk_size_bytes": 1882,
    "chunk_text": "Vital step in modernizing Sri Lanka\u2019s labour legislation\n\nA high-level committee appointed by the Cabinet to review and amend Sri Lanka\u2019s proposed Labour Act convened for the first time recently at the Ministry of Labour. The move marks a significant step towards modernizing labour legislation to address the evolving needs of the workforce across the state, semi-government and private sectors.\n\nThe meeting was chaired by S. M. Piyathissa, Secretary to the Ministry of Labour, with the participation of Labour Minister Dr. Anil Jayantha Fernando. The committee comprises top legal professionals, human resource and finance experts, Labour Commissioner General Nadika Watalyadda, and Additional Secretary (Development) Muditha Padmajay.\n\nDeputy Minister of Labour Mahinda Jayasinghe stressed the importance of the initiative, stating:\n\n\u201cThis reform process is critical to ensure Sri Lanka\u2019s labour laws meet global standards while remaining business-friendly. Our aim is to create a framework that safeguards workers\u2019 rights, improves productivity and enhances investor confidence in the Sri Lankan economy.\u201d\n\nThe government stressed that the amendments will align with International Labour Organization (ILO) conventions and be designed to reflect modern economic realities. The updated legal framework is also expected to strengthen tripartite relations between government, employers and employees, laying the groundwork for greater industrial stability.\n\nAccording to ministry officials, the committee is expected to complete its review and prepare a draft set of recommendations within the next three to six months. The government aims to table the revised Labour Act in Parliament before the end of the year, with stakeholder consultations\u2014including trade unions, employer federations and business chambers\u2014scheduled to take place during the drafting process."
