    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka stocks close up 0.23-pct, S&P SL20 dips\n\nECONOMYNEXT \u2013 The Colombo Stock Exchange\u2019s All Share Price Index (ASPI) closed 0.23 percent higher following a highly volatile session, brokers said.\n\n\u201cThere was volatility in the market. Sizeable upward trend in the morning was seen. Slight profit taking was there but it didn\u2019t last for too long,\u201d Dimantha Mathew, Chief Research and Strategy Officer at First Capital Holdings PLC said.\n\nThe ASPI climbed 47.05 points on Thursday to close at 20,800.26; while the S&P SL20 index dipped 0.27 percent, or 16.67 points, to close at 6,045.77.\n\n\u201cInvestors are moving into not so popular stocks. Not really from a particular sector as well,\u201d Mathew said. \u201cThese were mostly low market cap stocks; slightly lower levels of liquidity.\u201d\n\nThe ASPI was pushed higher on Dialog Axiata which ended 0.70 rupees up at 24.90; Ambeon Holdings which ended 14.25 rupees stronger at 132.75; Bukit Darah which ended 37.50 rupees up at 947.50; Carson Cumberbatch which moved 19.25 rupees down to 830.00; and LOLC Holdings closed up 6.50 rupees at 562.50.\n\nTurnover rose to 7.4 billion, while the share volume was 295,379,191.\n\nAlliance Finance Company PLC said it will issue listed, rated, unsecured, subordinated high yield bonds, to raise 1.5 billion rupees.\n\nElsewhere, the Pakistan Stock Exchange\u2019s KSE 100 Index was trading 0.09 percent weaker at 147,354.50.\n\nIndian equity markets fell due to concern over the \u201cimpact of US tariffs on Indian goods, a day after punitive duties went into effect,\u201d Reuters said.\n\nIndia\u2019s Nifty 50 fell 0.85 percent to 24,500.90, while the BSE Sensex was 0.87 percent up at 80,080.57.\n\nMeanwhile, Tokyo\u2019s Nikkei 225 index ended higher on artificial intelligence related stocks \u201cas positive results from US chip giant Nvidia Corp. improved confidence in the sector,\u201d Japan\u2019s The Mainichi newspaper said.\n\nThe index moved 0.73 percent, or 308.52 points higher, to 42,828.79."
