    "chunk_size_bytes": 1825,
    "chunk_text": "Too many ceremonies\n\nThe media is awash with stories about official ceremonies in Sri Lanka, which is perhaps the only country where no project is commenced or opened, without the presence of politicians. Even the opening of a public convenience is not considered complete without a politician cutting the so-called ceremonial ribbon. A member of the current administration has come under a social media piranha attack for ceremonially opening a public tap. During the last several decades, Presidents, Prime Ministers and ministers of all political hues have spent a lot of time, energy and state funds, attending ceremonies related to development projects, etc., countrywide.\n\nLast year, former Presidents Mahinda Rajapaksa and Maithripala Sirisena came under fire for their excessive travel in the Sri Lanka Air Force helicopters, while in office. The distance they, their predecessors and successors each travelled overland may be several times the circumference of the globe. It is doubtful whether any benefits have accrued to the country from their extensive travel. Former President Ranil Wickremesinghe is now facing legal action over alleged misuse of state funds during a foreign visit while in office.\n\nThe travel bug has not spared the members of the incumbent administration either despite their much-avowed commitment to austerity measures. They travel long distances to attend various ceremonies. President Anura Kumara Dissanayake was in the North yesterday to commence some development projects there. Northern development, no doubt, must figure high on the government\u2019s agenda, but should the President, ministers and state officials go all the way from Colombo to attend such events, for the public has to bear the cost of their travel, etc. Security arrangements alone cost the public an arm and a leg."
  },
